  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM EDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\n- Some frost across interior portions of Central Lower tonight\n\nWe have one more night in this series of cool nights where frost is \nlikely, mainly for the far NE portion of the area. This is the area \nwhere winds will remain lighter closer to the sfc ridge, and a lack \nof cloud cover will be late tonight. This does not look to be a \nmajor event as temps have warmed up a little as compared to last \nnight.\n\n- Small Chances of rain North early Tuesday and Tuesday night\n\nThe northern counties that do not look to have the frost potential \nwill see a bit more cloud cover overnight, and winds aloft \nincreasing as mentioned above. These factors will be the result of a \nwarm front that will be approaching that part of the area late \ntonight and first thing Tuesday morning. These are likely to be \nsprinkles more than anything else as the air below about 7-8k ft \nwill be rather dry. \n\nAfter a dry day on Tuesday, there will be another small chance of \nrain showers across the northern third of the forecast area. This is \nthe result of the cold front moving through and the associated upper \nwave barely grazing the area. The upper jet core stays just north of \nthe area, and therefore the best forcing and instability will stay \nnorth.\n\n- Mostly dry and increasingly warmer Wednesday and beyond\n\nThe upper wave on Wednesday moves by, but the lower heights remain \nentrenched across Lower Michigan and the region for one more day, \nbefore the long wave trough exits the region. This will bring cooler \ntemperatures for the area after a brief warm up Tuesday and \nWednesday.\n\nUpper heights will be rising then beginning Thursday night and will \ncontinue into early next week as a long wave upper ridge slowly \nbuilds our way. The building heights, and increasing return flow \nfrom the SSW bringing a warm front through Friday night will give \nthe temperatures a nice bump for next weekend. \n\nThe aforementioned warm front coming through Friday night could \nbring a small chance of brief light rain. Chances are that moisture \nwill be lacking in the lower to mid levels of the atmosphere. 850 mb \ntemperatures will be warming into the upper teens C, potentially \nallowing some areas to approach 80 F this weekend.\n\nThere is some uncertainty next Monday, regarding an upper trough \nthat may try to come north out of the Western Gulf. Right now the \nNBM blend is keeping the forecast dry. This could change depending \non how the long wave trough starts to move east and lifts it out.",
